<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Somapura Mahavihara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Paharpur, Paharpur Buddhist Vihara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Somapura <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavihara">Mahavihara</a> is the ruin of a great Buddhist monastery at Paharpur in Naogaon District, Bangladesh, founded by the Pala king Dharmapala in the late eighth century. Its square court of 177 monastic cells surrounds a cruciform temple whose plan influenced Buddhist architecture as far as Myanmar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. It was one of the largest monasteries south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1985.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/somapura-mahavihara.jpg" alt="Somapura Mahavihara" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abdulmominbd · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paharpur_Buddhist_Bihar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
